# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# The source cancionero left at M6 and nothing here reads a PDF any more. The rule stays
# anyway, because it was never about the extractor: public/ is served verbatim, so a
# tracked copy hands the whole book out at a guessable URL, and on a public repo the
# history would keep doing it long after the file was deleted. That is a fact about
# public/, so it outlives the tool. The book is at https://elukulelevene.co; if you want a
# copy, keep it outside the working tree.
/public/*.pdf

# The service worker, generated by `serwist build` — see serwist.config.js. It is one
# file now: `next-pwa` emitted a `workbox-<hash>.js` runtime beside it, and Serwist
# bundles the lot into sw.js. Those patterns are gone rather than kept "just in case",
# because an ignore rule for a file the tool cannot produce is a rule nobody can check.
# Anything they were still hiding is a leftover that should be deleted, not ignored.
/public/sw.js
/public/sw.js.map

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# The raw yt-dlp results behind data/videos.json, kept so the acceptance rule in
# scripts/find-videos.mjs can be re-applied without asking YouTube 276 more
# questions. It is a cache of somebody else's data and it goes stale; the file
# that is the record is data/videos.json, and it holds the four fields a person
# reviewing a match needs rather than everything the search returned.
/scripts/.videos-cache.json
